    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70052</id>
    <title>“Borrowing intelligence to build a business”: The promise of open‐source
                    &lt;scp&gt;AI&lt;/scp&gt;
                    for entrepreneurship</title>
    <updated>2026-10-23T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Georg von Krogh, Ann‐Kristin Weiser, Patrick Nicolas Tinguely</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Open‐source artificial intelligence (OSAI) is an increasingly important yet poorly understood frontier for entrepreneurial activity. We identify four defining features that shape new ventures' engagement with OSAI communities: dependence on sponsoring firms, layered licensing regimes, regulatory risks, and nation‐state influence. We develop the OSAI Framework for Market Entry to explain how open‐weight models relocate, rather than merely lower, entry barriers across six domains in AI‐enabled product and service markets. They may reduce resource barriers but create dependence on the governance of borrowed components, shifting the binding constraint from technical to commercial entry and value capture through complementary assets. We discuss contributions to research on entrepreneurial entry and strategy, private‐collective innovation, the openness paradox, and the new venture life cycle, and outline directions for future research.Managerial SummaryOpen‐source artificial intelligence (OSAI) offers new ventures access to powerful resources for developing AI‐enabled products and services without building AI capabilities from scratch. Yet new ventures must often contend with four key governance dependencies that shape how they engage with OSAI communities: reliance on large technology firms that contribute and steer critical layers of the AI stack, licensing terms that vary by layer, emerging regulatory requirements, and growing nation‐state influence over OSAI. Our OSAI Framework for Market Entry identifies where OSAI can lower entry barriers, where constraints shift instead, and how new ventures can manage the trade‐offs resulting from governance dependencies. It helps new ventures and investors evaluate when, where, and how to build on OSAI components, and which complementary assets can protect their returns.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70052"/>
    <category term="Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal"/>
    <published>2026-10-23T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhag091</id>
    <title>The Rise of Alternatives</title>
    <updated>2026-10-23T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Juliane Begenau, Pauline Liang, Emil Siriwardane</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;The Review of Financial Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Since the 2000s, U.S. public pension funds have actively shifted their risky investments away from public equities and toward alternative assets like private equity and hedge funds—some much more than others. We explore a range of possible explanations for these trends and argue that beliefs about the risk-adjusted return of alternatives have played a central role. Pension beliefs are shaped by investment consultants, peers, and experience in the 1990s. Other demand-side channels related to risk-seeking motives, spending needs, and fund governance have weaker empirical support. We also consider supply-side factors and agency interpretations of the data.</content>
