    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261504131</id>
    <title>What drives physician efficiency? Evidence from emergency department operations</title>
    <updated>2026-10-23T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Raha Imanirad, Soroush Saghafian, Stephen J Traub, Nicole R Hodgson</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Emergency department (ED) physicians play a critical role in healthcare. Yet increasing demand, resource constraints, and growing patient complexity pose significant challenges to physician efficiency. In this study, we investigate the determinants of ED physician efficiency using a multi-stage framework. First, we apply Data Envelopment Analysis to measure physician efficiency based on data from 110,325 patient visits at a leading U.S. hospital. Second, we use Tobit regression to analyze how physician experience, shift familiarity, and patient severity influence physician efficiency. Third, we conduct robustness checks, including omitted variable bias sensitivity analyses, permutation tests, and machine learning-based classification analyses, to address concerns regarding endogeneity and model misspecification. Our results show that physician efficiency increases with experience at a diminishing rate. Efficiency also varies with shift familiarity, with the estimated association weakening as physician experience increases. Higher patient severity is associated with lower physician efficiency, consistent with greater resource demands for higher-severity patients. This negative association is attenuated for more experienced physicians, suggesting that they are better able to maintain efficiency when managing more demanding clinical conditions. Accordingly, staffing strategies that assign relatively more experienced physicians to higher-severity patients may help maintain physician efficiency, while shift familiarity may inform a scheduling strategy that emphasizes greater scheduling consistency for relatively less experienced physicians and greater flexibility for relatively more experienced physicians. Simulation results illustrate the potential reductions in physician resource use associated with these strategies. Together, these findings highlight the value of evidence-based physician staffing and scheduling to improve physician efficiency in EDs.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261504131"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-10-23T00:00:00+00:00</published>
