    <title>Improving Competitiveness: A Freelancer-Centric Approach for Recommending Reskilling Strategies</title>
    <updated>2026-10-29T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Haoxin Liu, Shuo Yu, Stephen He, Wen Wen, Yidong Chai</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;As new technologies such as artificial intelligence (AI) rapidly reshape labor markets and the value of different skills, workers increasingly need to decide which skills to acquire to remain competitive. Yet most online labor platforms focus on helping employers find workers or workers find jobs, offering limited guidance on how workers can strategically reskill to improve their market competitiveness. We address this gap by developing an AI-enabled freelancer-centric skill advisor (FESA) that recommends skills based on freelancers’ desired tasks and supports both exploration (exploring new skills) and exploitation (deepening existing expertise) reskilling strategies. Using real-world data from a major online labor market, we show that FESA substantially outperforms existing approaches in identifying skills that improve freelancers’ competitiveness. A controlled experiment further shows that users are more motivated to pursue FESA’s personalized recommendations than generic popular-skill recommendations. Importantly, our experimental results reveal a key design lesson for AI-powered skill recommendation systems: More information is not always better. Revealing freelancers’ low market rankings can reduce their motivation to reskill, particularly under exploration reskilling strategies. Our study offers practical guidance for online platforms, workforce-development programs, and policymakers designing digital tools to support reskilling in rapidly changing labor markets.</content>
