    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2022.0411</id>
    <title>Catch Me if You Can! The Economic Analysis of Geofencing</title>
    <updated>2026-10-30T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Yi-Jen (Ian) Ho, Xiangjing (Olivia) Chen, Shengjun Mao</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Geofencing is widely used to reach nearby consumers, but our research shows that proximity alone should not determine where advertisers target. Using a game-theoretic model, we identify when geofencing is preferable to mass advertising and how businesses should configure it based on product attractiveness, consumer awareness, and location. When awareness is low, geofencing improves returns by concentrating advertising on the consumers most likely to respond. As awareness becomes uneven across locations, however, the best geofence may not be centered on the business itself. We introduce flexible geofencing, which allows advertisers to target a distant market when local consumers already know the business, but remote consumers do not. This strategy can generate higher advertising returns than traditional store-centered geofencing and, in most cases, increase consumer surplus and social welfare. The findings offer practical guidance for restaurants, retailers, parks, museums, healthcare providers, and public agencies seeking to allocate advertising budgets more efficiently. For policymakers, the analysis also highlights a potential exception: Remote targeting can sometimes reduce consumer surplus by overlooking uninformed local consumers, suggesting a role for initiatives that maintain local awareness.</content>
