    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261507274</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Partial or Full Doses First? Vaccine Allocation Under Limited Supply</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Ming Hu, Chaoyu Zhang, Yun Zhou</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Vaccines have proven highly effective in curbing disease spread in the face of sudden large-scale infectious diseases. However, the challenge of limited vaccine supplies during initial mass production or in certain regions such as Africa poses a significant hurdle. To address this issue, one potential solution is the implementation of a fractional dosing approach, whereby individuals receive partial doses of the vaccine to achieve partial immunity. In this paper, we investigate the optimal vaccine allocation policy in scenarios where the vaccine supply is scarce. By exploring the trade-offs between administering fractional and full dosages, we formulate the problem as an optimal control problem under disease transmission dynamics to minimize the total number of infections over a planning horizon. Specifically, we extend the Susceptible-Infected-Recovered model by introducing two new compartments for partially and fully vaccinated individuals. Under mild conditions, we demonstrate that an attainable optimal vaccine allocation policy exhibits a bang-bang structure, suggesting that either the Partial Doses First (PDF) or the Full Doses First (FDF) allocation policy achieves optimality at any time. Furthermore, we compare the committed PDF and FDF policies for the entire planning horizon and identify a critical threshold on the fractional-dose vaccine efficacy, such that when the efficacy is above (resp., below) this threshold, choosing the committed PDF (resp., FDF) policy is better. Using COVID-19 vaccination data, we calculate thresholds for different countries in January 2021 to recommend to governments whether the priority should be given to allocating the first dose or to prioritizing the allocation of both first and second doses to the population. We also illustrate the versatility of our proposed scarce capacity allocation model in addressing a broad spectrum of resource allocation problems under diffusion dynamics, for which partial resource allocation can still yield some, though not full, benefits and the optimal allocation decision involves balancing the coverage and protection level.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261507274"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261507272</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Optimizing Size-Based Bundle Promotions: Balancing Customer Appeal and Product Costs</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Ruijiu Mao, Shuai Jia, Chung-Piaw Teo</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Size-based bundle promotions (“p-for-s”) are widely used in retail to increase basket size, but designing them is challenging when product costs and customer preferences are heterogeneous. Customers favor high-value products; however, including such items increases the bundle’s cost and may require a higher price to sustain profitability. Existing models typically assume identical costs or uniform preferences and therefore largely overlook the interaction between cost heterogeneity and customer preferences. To examine this interaction, we begin with the “p-for-1” case under classical MNL and mixed MNL models and provide clear structural insights: (i) cost heterogeneity makes assortment selection nontrivial, motivating joint optimization of assortment and pricing; (ii) preference heterogeneity makes optimization under mixed MNL models computationally difficult; and (iii) extending MNL to “p-for-s” settings requires approximations and becomes intractable assgrows. Building on these insights, we develop an optimization framework for general “p-for-s” promotions with multiple concurrent offers. Our approach uses sample average approximation (SAA) to approximate the valuation distribution with a finite sample of customer valuations and reformulates the resulting problem as a discrete bilevel program that captures surplus-maximizing customer behavior. To improve computational tractability, we combine the SAA-based mixed-integer reformulation with low-rank completion, customer clustering, and consideration-set construction. Finally, a large-scale case study using data from a major e-commerce retailer shows that our framework yields substantially higher model-implied profit than the cost-threshold heuristic and outperforms other bundle-promotion benchmarks.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261507272"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261507271</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Customer-to-Manufacturer: Should a Platform Cooperate with More Manufacturers?</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Tonghuan Shi, Lin Tian, Zhen Xu</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Production and Operations Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;The traditional top-down relationship between manufacturers and consumers has led to persistently high failure rates in new product development. In response, the emerging customer-to-manufacturer (C2M) model leverages online platforms as intermediaries to directly transmit consumer feedback to manufacturers, thereby improving decision-making in product innovation and increasing the likelihood of product success. This paper develops an analytical model to study the strategic adoption of the C2M model in an online marketplace with one platform and two competing manufacturers. We focus on the role of platform information and examine how its trade-offs shape market outcomes. Our analysis shows that the impact of platform information on manufacturers' expected profits is non-monotonic. Moreover, manufacturers' cooperation decisions in C2M depend on the information price and are moderated by the amount of information. For the platform, unexpectedly, involving more manufacturers in C2M cooperation is not always optimal, especially when information is sufficient. We further show that the C2M model benefits the platform and consumers and improves social welfare, while potentially reducing manufacturers' profits. Overall, our results provide managerial and regulatory implications for platform-based C2M practices.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10591478261507271"/>
    <category term="Production and Operations Management"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T00:00:00+00:00</published>
