    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/00222437261507987</id>
    <title>EXPRESS: Are Exposure Bucks Worth It? Evidence from Instagram</title>
    <updated>2026-11-05T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Daniel Goetz, Andrew T. Ching</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Marketing Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We estimate the benefit to creators of making collaborative posts with brands on Instagram, a feature that allows brands and creators to co-author shared posts. We link all collaborative posts from 260 leading fashion and beauty brands to a daily panel of follower counts for small-to-mid-tier collaborating creators, match treated and control posts on visual content, and exploit rigidity in brands' posting schedules to identify causal treatment effects in a difference-in-differences design. Collaborative posts increase a creator's cumulative follower count by 0.52% on average—roughly equivalent to one week of organic growth—with no effect on follower engagement or creators' propensity to partner with other brands. Heterogeneity analyses suggest that the primary benefit stems from audience exposure rather than quality signaling. While brands with larger follower bases yield larger gains all else being equal, they also have less engaged audiences and publish collaborative content that deviates further from creators' typical styles; as a result, treatment effects remain roughly constant across brands ranging from 0.7 to 8.6 million followers. Overall, collaborative posts provide relatively small exposure benefits to creators on average, but partnerships with brands that have highly engaged audiences and grant creative freedom deliver substantially greater value.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/00222437261507987"/>
    <category term="Journal of Marketing Research"/>
    <published>2026-11-05T00:00:00+00:00</published>
